Point the built-in macros at this platform's compiler and link.

POSIX leaves the values of make's built-in macros implementation-defined, so
this is the port saying what its platform is rather than a change of behaviour.
Three of them are wrong for Rach2 as they stand.

CC is `cc` in the extensions mode, `c99` under POSIX 2017 and `c17` under POSIX
2024. This image carries one compiler, `tcc`, staged again as `/bin/cc` for a
student who types that; the other two names are on no path here, so a strict
POSIX build would fail with "not found" and teach the student something false
about their makefile. All three now name the compiler the machine has.

LDFLAGS is empty, so the built-in link rule would ask tcc for a dynamic
executable. tcc on this machine refuses that by its own pinned patch, because
this kernel has no dynamic linker and would load and jump into an image with
nothing relocated. `-static` is not an optimisation here; it is the only kind of
program that runs.

Without this a student's first `make` fails at the link, or at the compile if
they asked for a POSIX mode.

--- a/rules.c
+++ b/rules.c
@@ -241,18 +241,18 @@
 	"LFLAGS=\n" \
 	"AR=ar\n" \
 	"ARFLAGS=-rv\n" \
-	"LDFLAGS=\n"
+	"LDFLAGS=-static\n"
 
 #define MACROS_2017 \
-	"CC=c99\n" \
+	"CC=tcc\n" \
 	"FC=fort77\n" \
 	"FFLAGS=-O1\n" \
 
 #define MACROS_2024 \
-	"CC=c17\n"
+	"CC=tcc\n"
 
 #define MACROS_EXT \
-	"CC=cc\n"
+	"CC=tcc\n"
 
 /*
  * Read the built-in rules using a fake fgets-like interface.
